The policy requires that the native method not be declared public but be declared private and exposed through a public Java wrapper that performs checks before calling the native method. The wrapper must make a defensive copy of mutable input (e.g., data = data.clone()) and validate offset and length in an overflow-safe way (do not rely on computing offset+len). The example validation shown is:

if (offset < 0 || len < 0 || offset > data.length - len) {
    throw new IllegalArgumentException();
}

Only after these checks should the wrapper invoke the private native method.
